+# Block placement: performance review
+
+Files reviewed: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau` and
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockPlacer.luau`.
+
+## The load
+
+- The server accepts at most one placement per player every 0.1 s (`MIN_INTERVAL`), so 10 per
+  second per player.
+- A server holds 8 players, so the worst case is **80 placements a second** server-wide. Real
+  play is far below that.
+- Each placement costs a few type checks, a hash lookup, one `Vector3` distance, and creating
+  one anchored `Part`. That's microseconds of Luau plus one part, about 80 times a second at
+  most, which is well under 1% of a 16.7 ms frame.
+- Each client sends one `PlaceBlock` call per click: a short string plus a `Vector3`, a few
+  dozen bytes. At 10 a second that's well under 1 KB/s per player.
+- Nobody has reported lag, and no profile points at this code.
+
+So none of the six items fixes a measured problem, and the estimate says none of them could
+make a difference players would notice. The rule I used: a change goes in only if it fixes a
+measured cost, or if it makes the code simpler without changing behavior. Only item 5 passes,
+and it passes because it's simpler, not because it's faster.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+### 1. Pool the block Parts: **rejected**
+
+Pooling saves time only when instances are destroyed and then created again, because a pool
+recycles the destroyed ones. Placed blocks are never destroyed: nothing in the code removes
+them. A pool would have nothing to recycle, so it would just be a stack of pre-made parts.
+Those spare parts would sit on the server, replicate to every client on join, and need
+property resets (name, color, material, transparency, `PlacedBy`) to avoid stale-state bugs.
+Creating at most 80 parts a second is not a cost anyone has measured.
+
+Revisit if blocks get a break/remove feature **and** a MicroProfiler capture shows part
+creation in spike frames.
+
+### 2. Pack PlaceBlock's arguments into a buffer: **rejected**
+
+The payload is one short string and one `Vector3`, a few dozen bytes per click at no more than
+10 clicks a second. A buffer would save maybe a dozen bytes per call. Buffers save bytes and
+memory, not time, and that's worth it only for high-volume traffic like per-frame position
+streams. Here it would add encoding code on the client, plus decoding and bounds checks in a
+server handler that currently relies on the simple `typeof` checks. That's more code where
+client input gets validated, for a saving nobody can measure.
+
+### 3. Make PlaceBlock an UnreliableRemoteEvent: **rejected (this would break the game)**
+
+Unreliable events don't arrive faster. They're allowed to drop or reorder packets, and they
+drop immediately when the send budget fills. That suits streams where a newer value replaces
+a lost one (aim direction, positions). A placement is a one-time command: with an unreliable
+event, some clicks would place nothing, with no error, and the bug would show up most on bad
+connections. This changes behavior, not just speed.
+
+### 4. Add `--!native` to both scripts: **rejected**
+
+Native code generation speeds up loops that do a lot of number crunching in Luau. These scripts
+mostly call engine APIs (`Instance.new`, property writes, `Raycast`, `FireServer`), and native
+code doesn't make those faster. The handler runs at most 80 times a second. Native code also
+uses compile time and memory, and that memory limit is shared by the whole game. On the client
+it's officially supported only on Android, so it may do nothing on other live clients. Use it
+only on a function a profile names.
+
+### 5. Replace the `table.find` check with a lookup table: **accepted, as a cleanup**
+
+There's no real speed gain. `table.find` over 5 names takes nanoseconds, and the set-vs-scan
+gap matters with hundreds or thousands of entries. But the lookup table already exists:
+`BlockTypes.ByName`, which the handler was reading right after the `table.find` check. The
+server built a second copy of the names (`VALID_NAMES`) only to scan it. The handler now checks
+`BlockTypes.ByName[blockTypeName]` directly and returns when it's `nil`, and `VALID_NAMES` is
+gone. It accepts and rejects exactly the same names as before (`ByName` is a frozen plain table
+with no metatable, so any client string is safe to index with). The win is one less copy of the
+list to keep in sync.
+
+### 6. Create RaycastParams once instead of on every click: **rejected**
+
+Creating a `RaycastParams` costs microseconds, and it happens once per click. The filter holds
+the player's character, which changes on every respawn, so a shared params object would still
+need `FilterDescendantsInstances` set on every click. All you'd save is one small allocation
+per click, in exchange for module-level state that can go stale. Reusing params is worth it for
+raycasts that run every frame or many times a frame, not for one per click.
+
+Revisit if placement gets a per-frame preview (a ghost block that follows the mouse). That
+raycast would run every frame, and building the params once would be the normal way to write it.
+
+## What would change this
+
+If players report lag while building, measure before changing anything: a MicroProfiler capture
+on the server and a client during heavy building, and `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` on the server. The
+likely costs at scale aren't in this code. They're the total number of parts in the world
+(memory, join size, rendering), which no change on this list addresses.
